%I A402008 #9 Nov 18 2026 15:18:03
%S A402008 7565,728533,3088577,51229109,124946077,176106521,961651133,
%T A402008 1197354421,5144642953,11715789209,21379025137,15382013261,
%U A402008 36026885029,23910348113,119173790297,135869208269,221758349077,269284790213,239057770141,595582421233,391942524317
%N A402008 Square roots of the central entries in a six-square 3 X 3 magic-square construction from Pythagorean primes using fourth powers of the hypotenuse and the sum of the legs.
%C A402008 For p = A002144(n), let q = A402007(n) and Q = a(n) = p*q.
%C A402008 There are unique integers x > y > 0 such that p = x^2 + y^2. Let u = x^2 - y^2, v = 2*x*y and h = u+v. Here u is the odd leg and v is the even leg of the primitive Pythagorean triangle with hypotenuse p.
%C A402008 Let C = Q^2, X = p^3*h^2, Y = p*(h^4-p^4)/2, d = 2*u*v*q^2, e = 2*X*Y and R = h*(h^4-3*p^4)/2.
%C A402008 Then the array with rows
%C A402008   [C-e,     C+d, C-d+e],
%C A402008   [C-d+2*e, C,   C+d-2*e],
%C A402008   [C+d-e,   C-d, C+e]
%C A402008 is a 3 X 3 magic square with magic sum 3*C. The six entries C-e, C+d, C, C+d-2*e, C-d and C+e are respectively (X-Y)^2, (q*h)^2, Q^2, R^2, (q*(u-v))^2 and (X+Y)^2.
%C A402008 All nine entries are positive and distinct; see the linked note for the proof. The construction works whether q is prime or composite.
%C A402008 The upper left entry is A400365(n)^2, since X-Y = p*(u^4+v^4).
%C A402008 The terms are listed in increasing order of p, not in increasing order of Q; for example, a(11) = 21379025137 > a(12) = 15382013261.
%C A402008 Since gcd(p,q) = 1, Q is a product of two distinct primes exactly when the corresponding p belongs to A402006.
%C A402008 For background on 3 X 3 magic squares with six square entries, see Morgenstern's discussion of Euler's formulas and Boyer's work linked below.
%H A402008 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402008/b402008.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..2549</a>
%H A402008 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquares.htm">Magic squares of squares</a>.
%H A402008 Christian Boyer, <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02985794">Some notes on the magic squares of squares problem</a>, The Mathematical Intelligencer, 27 (2005), 52-64.
%H A402008 Christian Boyer, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/SquaresOfSquaresSearch.htm">Latest research on the "3x3 magic square of squares" problem</a>.
%H A402008 Lee Morgenstern, <a href="http://www.multimagie.com/English/Morgenstern14.htm">Euler's Formulas for 3x3 Magic Squares of 6 Squares</a>, December 2011.
%H A402008 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A402008/a402008.txt">Half-sums of fourth powers from Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402008 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A400365/a400365.txt">Fourth powers of Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402008 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401004/a401004.txt">Pythagorean primes, a Pell-type recurrence and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402008 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401446/a401446.txt">A Fibonacci recurrence step on Pythagorean legs and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%H A402008 Edward Schmidt, <a href="/A401742/a401742.txt">Semiperimeter and inradius of Pythagorean triangles and a magic square with six squares</a>.
%F A402008 If p = A002144(n) and u, v are the legs of its primitive Pythagorean triangle, then a(n) = p*(p^4+(u+v)^4)/2.
%F A402008 a(n) = A002144(n)*A402007(n).
%e A402008 For p = 5, q = (5^4+7^4)/2 = 1513 and a(1) = 5*1513 = 7565. The construction gives
%e A402008   [    1685^2, 10591^2,  56679169 ]
%e A402008   [ 111069169,  7565^2,    1841^2 ]
%e A402008   [  57779281,  1513^2,   10565^2 ]
%e A402008 Every row, column and diagonal sums to 3*7565^2 = 171687675. All nine entries are positive and distinct, and exactly six of them are squares.
%e A402008 For p = 13, q = (13^4+17^4)/2 = 56041 is prime and a(2) = 13*56041 = 728533; the same construction again gives a magic square with six square entries.
%o A402008 (Python)
%o A402008 from math import isqrt
%o A402008 from sympy import primerange
%o A402008 def a402008(limit=200):
%o A402008     # Return terms for hypotenuses p < limit.
%o A402008     out = []
%o A402008     for p in primerange(5, limit):
%o A402008         if p % 4 != 1:
%o A402008             continue
%o A402008         for y in range(1, isqrt(p // 2) + 1):
%o A402008             x = isqrt(p - y*y)
%o A402008             if x*x + y*y == p:
%o A402008                 h = x*x - y*y + 2*x*y
%o A402008                 out.append(p*((p**4 + h**4) // 2))
%o A402008                 break
%o A402008     return out
%Y A402008 Cf. A002144, A002365, A002366, A400363, A400364, A400365, A401002, A401003, A401004, A401444, A401445, A401446, A401740, A401741, A401742, A402006, A402007.
%K A402008 nonn,easy,new
%O A402008 1,1
%A A402008 _Edward Schmidt_, Nov 12 2026
